  // ==========================================================================
  // Wave U (paideia-os #2449) -- VFS metadata block completeness follow-up
  //   + POSIX-shape floor landings (rmdir, lseek, fstat, mmap, pipe,
  //   sigaction).
  //
  // sys_rmdir closes the exact gap Wave 7 (#2445)'s own header comment
  // flagged: "rmdir @80 / rename @82 remain out of scope for Wave 7;
  // follow-up filed." The kernel dispatch arm (dispatch_rmdir, SC+ ID
  // 80) has existed since R56.M3-001 (#1790); only the userspace
  // trampoline was missing.
  //
  // sys_unlink is NOT re-added here -- it already exists above at SC+
  // ID 81 (Wave 7, #2445). The #2449 issue text proposed ID 87 for it,
  // but 87 is already SC+ ID for sys_socket (R72.M1-005, #1927,
  // landed earlier in this same file); reusing 87 would collide. The
  // existing sys_unlink wrapper at 81 is the correct, already-landed
  // one -- no action needed.
  //
  // lseek (8), mmap (9), pipe (22) keep the issue's requested POSIX-
  // shape sysnos verbatim -- all three are genuinely free in the
  // kernel's dispatch table (`cmp rdi, N` chain in core/syscall/
  // dispatch.pdx never emits 8, 9, or 22).
  //
  // fstat and sigaction could NOT keep their issue-requested numbers:
  //   - fstat requested sysno 5, but 5 is reserved for SYS_YIELD by
  //     paideia-os/shell#47 (the shell satellite's Syscall module names
  //     it explicitly; see tools/user/shell/src/syscall.pdx SYS_YIELD).
  //     Landing fstat at 5 here would collide with that shell-visible
  //     reservation the moment either lands a real kernel dispatch arm.
  //   - sigaction requested sysno 13, but 13 is SYS_DMESG (logging-m9-
  //     002, #709), already dispatched in core/syscall/dispatch.pdx.
  // Both shift to the next free contiguous block after the highest
  // sysno any wrapper in this file names (115, Wave 7's sys_semantic_
  // send) -- the same "IDs are negotiable, shift to the next free slot"
  // resolution R53.M2-001's own sys_mount/sys_umount comment documents
  // for an analogous collision. fstat lands at 116, sigaction at 117.
  // shell#39's sys_exec_reconcile_caps (118) and #2455's sys_clock_ticks
  // (119) continue the same contiguous "Wave U" block for the same
  // reason (both also collided with their issue-requested numbers,
  // which were already claimed by sys_sendto/sys_recvfrom).
  //
  // NONE of the six sysnos below (80 excepted -- dispatch_rmdir is
  // real) have a kernel dispatch arm yet: `cmp rdi, 115; ja dispatch_
  // enosys` in core/syscall/dispatch.pdx bounds every sysno above 115
  // to -ENOSYS today. This is the same floor-first / consumer-later
  // staging pattern SYS_YIELD (shell#47) and the R53.M2-001 sys_mount/
  // sys_umount forward-declaration stubs both used: land the userspace
  // trampoline now so the SHAPE is frozen and reviewable, wire the
  // kernel dispatch arm in a follow-up. A caller invoking any of
  // sys_lseek/sys_fstat/sys_mmap/sys_pipe/sys_sigaction today observes
  // -ENOSYS until that follow-up lands.
  // ==========================================================================

  // sys_rmdir(path_va, path_len) -> 0 or negative errno.
  pub let sys_rmdir : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{fs} =
    fn (path_va: u64) (path_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {fs},
      justification: "Wave U (#2449): SC+ ID 80. SysV args rdi/rsi -> SYSCALL ID 80. No arg shuffle (arity 2 < 4). Kernel body: dispatch_rmdir has existed in core/syscall/dispatch.pdx since R56.M3-001 (#1790); this trampoline closes the userspace-wrapper gap Wave 7 (#2445)'s own header comment flagged as a filed follow-up. Same shape as the sibling sys_mkdir/sys_unlink wrappers above.",
      block: {
        mov rax, 80;
        syscall;
        ret
      }
    }

